---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import '../../styles/hidden-rivers.css';
import katex from 'katex';
import { LANDSCAPE_PLACES } from '../../scripts/hidden-rivers/places.mjs';
const plates = [
 ['agulhas-0-speed','The Agulhas turn','Surface currents south of Africa','agulhas',0,'flow'],
 ['bahamas-200-speed','Through the Florida Straits','Horizontal currents at 200 metres','bahamas',200,'flow'],
 ['denmark-1000-speed','Below Denmark Strait','Horizontal currents at 1,000 metres','denmark',1000,'flow'],
 ['agulhas-vorticity','The sign of rotation','Clockwise and counterclockwise surface rotation','agulhas',0,'vorticity'],
 ['agulhas-ftle','Where neighboring paths separate','48-hour forward stretching, 1–3 October 2026','agulhas',0,'stretching'],
 ['cape-tessera','A reference in the Cape Peninsula','2024 Tessera similarity beside satellite context','cape',0,'tessera'],
];
const math=(s:string)=>katex.renderToString(s,{throwOnError:false,displayMode:true});
---
<Base wide title="Hidden Rivers | ocean transport and coastal representations" description="Trace modeled ocean transport, inspect rotation and finite-time stretching, and compare coastal satellite representations in a linked 3D atlas." pageClass="hidden-rivers-page">
<article class="ocean-observatory" data-surface="ocean">
  <section class="ocean-stage" id="explorer" aria-label="Ocean transport observatory">
    <div id="ocean-canvas" class="ocean-canvas" role="img" aria-label="Interactive geospatial ocean-current visualization. Use the controls or location inspector below."></div>
    <div class="stage-shade" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <div class="stage-heading"><p class="eyebrow">IAN HELFRICH <span>/</span> OCEANOGRAPHIC SYSTEMS</p><h1>Hidden <em>Rivers</em></h1><p class="stage-deck">The ocean’s submerged currents.</p><p class="stage-evidence" id="ocean-evidence">HYCOM · NOAA · Tessera · Cesium</p><a class="stage-descend" href="#map-room">The map room</a></div>
    <div class="stage-topline"><div class="region-switch" role="group" aria-label="Ocean region"><button type="button" data-region="agulhas" aria-pressed="true"><span>01</span> Agulhas Current</button><button type="button" data-region="bahamas" aria-pressed="false"><span>02</span> Florida Straits</button><button type="button" data-region="denmark" aria-pressed="false"><span>03</span> Denmark Strait</button></div><a class="stage-archive" href="/projects/hidden-rivers">Project record ↗</a></div>
    <div class="stage-location"><span class="location-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span><div><span id="ocean-region-title">Agulhas retroflection</span><small id="ocean-bounds">SOUTH ATLANTIC / INDIAN OCEAN</small></div></div>
    <div class="stage-tools"><label class="ocean-speed-control">Speed scale<select id="ocean-speed-scale"><option value="0.25">0–0.25 m/s</option><option value="0.5" selected>0–0.5 m/s</option><option value="1">0–1 m/s</option><option value="2">0–2 m/s</option></select></label><label class="ocean-surface-control"><input id="ocean-surface" type="checkbox"/> Surface image</label><button type="button" id="ocean-view" aria-pressed="false">Oblique view</button><button type="button" id="ocean-reset">Fit region</button><button type="button" id="ocean-image">Save image ↗</button><button type="button" id="ocean-share">Share view ↗</button><button type="button" id="ocean-immersive">Full screen</button><button type="button" data-present="ocean">Presentation mode</button></div>
    <div class="stage-legend"><span id="ocean-legend-title">Horizontal speed</span><div id="ocean-gradient"></div><div class="legend-ticks" id="ocean-legend-ticks"><span>0</span><span>1</span><span>2 m/s</span></div><small id="ocean-legend-note">Color encodes speed. Light does not encode water volume.</small></div>
    <div class="stage-loading" id="ocean-status" role="status"><span class="loading-line" aria-hidden="true"></span><p>Loading ocean fields</p></div>
    <div class="stage-caption"><span id="ocean-path-info">Deterministic model trajectories</span><span id="ocean-camera-note">Drag to pan · Scroll to zoom · Click to inspect</span><span class="imagery-credit">Imagery © Esri, Maxar, Earthstar Geographics</span></div>
    <div class="stage-depth-readout"><span>DEPTH BELOW SEA LEVEL</span><strong id="ocean-depth-readout">1,000 <small>m</small></strong><p id="ocean-depth-context">Horizontal flow in the ocean interior</p></div><div class="stage-bottom">
      <div class="mode-switch" role="group" aria-label="Analysis layer"><button type="button" data-mode="flow" aria-pressed="true"><span>01</span>Transport<small>Particle paths</small></button><button type="button" data-mode="vorticity" aria-pressed="false"><span>02</span>Rotation<small>Signed vorticity</small></button><button type="button" data-mode="stretching" aria-pressed="false"><span>03</span>Deformation<small>48-hour stretching</small></button><button type="button" data-mode="column" aria-pressed="false"><span>04</span>Water column<small>Compare the depth layers</small></button></div>
      <div class="playback-row"><button id="ocean-play" class="play-button" type="button" aria-pressed="true">Pause</button><div class="time-control"><div><output id="ocean-date">2 October 2026, 00:00 UTC</output><span id="ocean-cadence">Source cadence</span></div><input id="ocean-time" type="range" min="0" max="432000" step="900" value="259200" aria-label="Model time"/><div class="time-extents"><span>29 SEPTEMBER</span><span id="ocean-time-end">4 OCTOBER 2026</span></div></div><div class="depth-switch" id="ocean-depths" role="group" aria-label="Current depth"><button data-depth="0" type="button">Surface</button><button data-depth="200" type="button" aria-pressed="true">200 m</button><button data-depth="1000" type="button">1,000 m</button></div></div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <nav class="observatory-nav" aria-label="Atlas sections"><a href="#explorer">Ocean</a><a href="#remote-atlas">Remote atlas</a><a href="#map-room">Model plates</a><a href="#landscapes">24 places</a><a href="#coastal">Tessera</a><a href="#cinema">Film</a><a href="#methods">Methods</a><button type="button" data-present="ocean">Present</button><span id="ocean-action-status" role="status"></span></nav>
  <section class="analysis-reading" id="ocean-analysis" aria-labelledby="analysis-title">
    <div class="analysis-copy"><p class="eyebrow" id="ocean-mode-kicker">01 / LAGRANGIAN TRANSPORT</p><h2 id="analysis-title">Follow a parcel through a changing current.</h2><p id="ocean-mode-description">Each filament is a numerical trajectory through the model’s evolving horizontal velocity field. Its history is measured in ocean hours, independent of the display’s frame rate. Scrubbing time follows the same seeded particles.</p><p class="analysis-limit" id="ocean-mode-limit">Particles stay on a selected depth surface. These paths do not include vertical motion, turbulent diffusion, waves, or windage.</p><div class="analysis-links"><button type="button" id="ocean-film-camera">Slow camera orbit</button><a href="#methods">Inspect the calculation ↗</a></div></div>
    <div class="column-inspector"><div class="inspector-heading"><p class="eyebrow">VELOCITY / SELECTED LOCATION</p><h3 id="ocean-inspect-title">Choose a location in the ocean.</h3><p id="ocean-inspect-time"></p></div><form id="ocean-inspect-form"><label>Longitude °E<input type="number" step="any" id="ocean-lon" value="26" min="-180" max="180" required/></label><label>Latitude °N<input type="number" step="any" id="ocean-lat" value="-36" min="-90" max="90" required/></label><button type="submit">Inspect ↗</button></form><p id="ocean-inspect-message" role="status"></p><div class="inspect-table"><table><caption>Horizontal velocity · m/s</caption><thead><tr><th>Depth</th><th>Speed</th><th>East</th><th>North</th><th>Toward</th></tr></thead><tbody id="ocean-inspect-body"><tr><td colspan="5">Click the field above or enter coordinates.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><svg id="ocean-profile" role="img" aria-label="Modeled speed over time at the selected location"></svg><div id="ocean-profile-legend"></div><button id="ocean-csv" type="button" disabled>Download point series</button><p class="fineprint">Directions point toward motion, clockwise from true north. Missing values remain missing. Depth colors identify the plotted series; they do not encode speed.</p></div>
  </section>
  <section id="remote-atlas" class="map-room" aria-labelledby="remote-atlas-title">
    <header class="section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">SATELLITE SCENES / OCEAN COLOR / COASTLINES</p><h2 id="remote-atlas-title">The ocean,<br/><em>in its own colors.</em></h2><p>Satellite views set beside measured ocean fields. Each image keeps its date, spatial scale, source, and limits in view.</p></header>
    <div class="atlas-plates remote-atlas-plates">
      <figure class="atlas-plate atlas-plate-lead"><a href="/hidden-rivers/remote-atlas/global-drifter-currents.webp" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="/hidden-rivers/remote-atlas/global-drifter-currents.webp" width="2400" height="1350" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Global annual-mean near-surface current speed and sampled direction, derived from NOAA satellite-tracked drifter climatology at a drogue depth of 15 metres."/></a><figcaption><span class="plate-number">01</span><div><h3>The currents that wrap the world</h3><p>A global seasonal view derived from observed drifting-buoy velocities, with the drogue centered at 15 m. This is a climatology through February 2023, not a real-time map. Animated particles trace the mean fields for illustration; they are not measured drifter tracks.</p></div><div class="plate-actions"><button type="button" data-present="global-currents" data-presentation-image="global-currents" data-presentation-src="/hidden-rivers/remote-atlas/global-drifter-currents-visual.webp" data-presentation-alt="Global drifter-derived near-surface current climatology">Present</button><a href="/hidden-rivers/remote-atlas/global-drifter-currents.json">Methods & source ↗</a><a href="https://www.aoml.noaa.gov/phod/gdp/mean_velocity.php" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NOAA drifter archive ↗</a></div></figcaption></figure>
      <figure class="atlas-plate atlas-plate-lead"><a href="/hidden-rivers/remote-atlas/blue-hole-sentinel-pca.webp" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="/hidden-rivers/remote-atlas/blue-hole-sentinel-pca.webp" width="2400" height="1500" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Sentinel-2 surface-reflectance principal components over Lighthouse Reef and the Great Blue Hole. PC3, PC2, and PC1 form the displayed red, green, and blue channels."/></a><figcaption><span class="plate-number">02</span><div><h3>Light through four bands</h3><p>True PCA of cloud-screened Sentinel-2 surface reflectance: blue, green, red, and near-infrared. PC3 / PC2 / PC1 are shown as RGB; stretch is percentile-based. This is spectral structure, not a coral-health classification.</p></div><div class="plate-actions"><button type="button" data-present="blue-hole-pca" data-presentation-image="blue-hole-pca" data-presentation-src="/hidden-rivers/remote-atlas/blue-hole-sentinel-pca-visual.webp" data-presentation-alt="Sentinel-2 multispectral PCA over Lighthouse Reef, Belize">Present</button><a href="/hidden-rivers/remote-atlas/sentinel-pca.json">Scene & variance ↗</a><a href="https://earth-search.aws.element84.com/v1/collections/sentinel-2-l2a" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sentinel-2 source ↗</a></div></figcaption></figure>
      <figure class="atlas-plate"><a href="/hidden-rivers/remote-atlas/great-barrier-reef.webp" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="/hidden-rivers/remote-atlas/great-barrier-reef.webp" width="2400" height="1500" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Great Barrier Reef satellite imagery alongside NOAA Coral Reef Watch degree heating week stress field."/></a><figcaption><span class="plate-number">02</span><div><h3>Heat held in the reef</h3><p>Great Barrier Reef, with NOAA’s satellite-derived cumulative heat stress beside the natural-color scene. The colors describe thermal exposure, not observed coral condition.</p></div><div class="plate-actions"><button type="button" data-present="gbr-heat" data-presentation-image="gbr-heat" data-presentation-src="/hidden-rivers/remote-atlas/great-barrier-reef-visual.webp" data-presentation-alt="Great Barrier Reef satellite image and NOAA heat stress field">Present</button><a href="https://coralreefwatch.noaa.gov/product/5km/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NOAA data ↗</a></div></figcaption></figure>
      <figure class="atlas-plate"><a href="/hidden-rivers/remote-atlas/blue-hole.webp" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="/hidden-rivers/remote-atlas/blue-hole.webp" width="2400" height="1500" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Satellite view of Lighthouse Reef, Belize, centered on the Great Blue Hole."/></a><figcaption><span class="plate-number">03</span><div><h3>The Blue Hole, held in the reef</h3><p>Aerial context for the circular collapse feature and its surrounding atoll.</p></div><div class="plate-actions"><button type="button" data-present="blue-hole" data-presentation-image="blue-hole" data-presentation-src="/hidden-rivers/remote-atlas/blue-hole-visual.webp" data-presentation-alt="Lighthouse Reef and the Great Blue Hole from above">Present</button><a href="https://asterweb.jpl.nasa.gov/gallery-detail.asp?name=bluehole" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NASA / ASTER ↗</a></div></figcaption></figure>
      <figure class="atlas-plate"><a href="/hidden-rivers/remote-atlas/outer-banks.webp" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="/hidden-rivers/remote-atlas/outer-banks.webp" width="2400" height="1500" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Outer Banks and Cape Hatteras from satellite imagery."/></a><figcaption><span class="plate-number">04</span><div><h3>Outer Banks, shifting edge</h3><p>Barrier islands, inlets, and shoals. This scene is geographic context, not an erosion-rate estimate.</p></div><div class="plate-actions"><button type="button" data-present="outer-banks" data-presentation-image="outer-banks" data-presentation-src="/hidden-rivers/remote-atlas/outer-banks-visual.webp" data-presentation-alt="The Outer Banks and Cape Hatteras from above">Present</button><a href="https://www.fisheries.noaa.gov/inport/item/72201" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NOAA shoreline record ↗</a></div></figcaption></figure>
      <figure class="atlas-plate"><a href="/hidden-rivers/remote-atlas/amazon-plume.webp" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="/hidden-rivers/remote-atlas/amazon-plume.webp" width="2400" height="1500" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Amazon plume satellite image beside NOAA CoastWatch SMAP surface salinity on 15 March 2024."/></a><figcaption><span class="plate-number">05</span><div><h3>Fresh water meeting salt</h3><p>Amazon plume imagery alongside one NOAA SMAP daily surface-salinity field at its native 0.25° grid. Near-land retrievals are less reliable; this broad view does not resolve estuaries.</p></div><div class="plate-actions"><button type="button" data-present="amazon-salinity" data-presentation-image="amazon-salinity" data-presentation-src="/hidden-rivers/remote-atlas/amazon-plume-visual.webp" data-presentation-alt="Amazon plume and satellite-informed surface salinity">Present</button><a href="https://oceanwatch.noaa.gov/cwn/products/sea-surface-salinity-near-real-time-smap.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NOAA SMAP ↗</a></div></figcaption></figure>
      <figure class="atlas-plate"><a href="/hidden-rivers/remote-atlas/point-nemo.webp" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="/hidden-rivers/remote-atlas/point-nemo.webp" width="2400" height="1500" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="South Pacific near Point Nemo, the oceanic pole of inaccessibility."/></a><figcaption><span class="plate-number">06</span><div><h3>Water in every direction</h3><p>Point Nemo sits far from land beneath the South Pacific gyre.</p></div><div class="plate-actions"><button type="button" data-present="point-nemo" data-presentation-image="point-nemo" data-presentation-src="/hidden-rivers/remote-atlas/point-nemo-visual.webp" data-presentation-alt="The open South Pacific at Point Nemo">Present</button><a href="https://oceanservice.noaa.gov/facts/nemo.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NOAA location ↗</a></div></figcaption></figure>
      <figure class="atlas-plate"><a href="/hidden-rivers/remote-atlas/guiana-shield.webp" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img src="/hidden-rivers/remote-atlas/guiana-shield.webp" width="2400" height="1500" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Forest and river corridor near the proposed Amaila Falls hydropower site in Guyana."/></a><figcaption><span class="plate-number">07</span><div><h3>Amaila Falls, Guyana</h3><p>Forest and river country around the proposed site. The cited government records do not establish an operating dam or that it is unregulated.</p></div><div class="plate-actions"><button type="button" data-present="amaila" data-presentation-image="amaila" data-presentation-src="/hidden-rivers/remote-atlas/guiana-shield-visual.webp" data-presentation-alt="The proposed Amaila Falls site in Guyana's forested interior">Present</button><a href="https://dpi.gov.gy/cabinet-grants-no-objection-to-amaila-falls-hydropower-project-afhp-and-the-new-demerara-harbour-bridge-ndhb/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Guyana DPI record ↗</a></div></figcaption></figure>
    </div>
    <p class="fineprint remote-atlas-note">Satellite basemap: Esri World Imagery (imagery dates vary). PCA input: Copernicus Sentinel-2 Level-2A, 10 m bands; colors are principal components, not natural color. NOAA SMAP salinity is a daily satellite-informed Level-3 field on a 0.25° grid. NOAA Coral Reef Watch DHW is a 5 km satellite-derived thermal-stress product.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="map-room" class="map-room" aria-labelledby="map-room-title">
    <header class="section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">SIX MAPS / DOWNLOADABLE AT 2,800 × 2,000</p><h2 id="map-room-title">Model fields<br/><em>in stills.</em></h2><p>Current direction, speed, rotation, and stretching come from the released HYCOM fields. Every plate includes its own scale, date, depth, and projection.</p></header>
    <p class="presentation-help">Presentation mode: ← → to change visuals · Space to pause · Esc to return. Move the pointer or tap for controls.</p><div class="atlas-plates">{plates.map(([id,title,caption,region,depth,mode],i)=><figure class:list={['atlas-plate',{'atlas-plate-lead':i===0}]}>
      <a href={`/hidden-rivers/plates/${id}.png`} target="_blank" rel="noopener" aria-label={`Open full-resolution map: ${title}`}><img src={`/hidden-rivers/plates/${id}.webp`} width="1400" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt={`${title}. ${caption}. Quantitative scale, geographic coordinates, and sources appear in the map.`}/></a>
      <figcaption><span class="plate-number">{String(i+1).padStart(2,'0')}</span><div><h3>{title}</h3><p>{caption}</p></div><div class="plate-actions"><button type="button" data-present={id} data-presentation-image={id} data-presentation-src={`/hidden-rivers/plates/${id}-presentation.webp`} data-presentation-alt={`${title}. Text-free presentation view.`}>Present</button><a href={`/hidden-rivers/plates/${id}.png`} download>Download PNG</a><a href={mode==='tessera'?'#coastal':`/hidden-rivers/research/?region=${region}&depth=${depth}&mode=${mode}&scale=2&time=${mode==='flow'?259200:172800}#explorer`}>Open field</a></div></figcaption>
    </figure>)}</div>
  </section>
  <section id="landscapes" class="landscape-section" aria-labelledby="landscape-title">
    <header class="section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">24 PLACES / CESIUM WORLD TERRAIN & OSM BUILDINGS</p><h2 id="landscape-title">Rivers meet<br/><em>the built world.</em></h2><p>From Fayetteville on the Cape Fear to the mountains above Cape Town. Terrain and available building geometry give the landscape its shape; aerial imagery supplies surface detail.</p></header>
    <div class="landscape-controls"><label>Location<select id="landscape-place">{[...new Set(LANDSCAPE_PLACES.map(p=>p.group))].map(group=><optgroup label={group}>{LANDSCAPE_PLACES.filter(p=>p.group===group).map(p=><option value={p.id} selected={p.id==='fayetteville'}>{p.label}</option>)}</optgroup>)}</select></label><button id="ocean-open-coast" type="button">Open 3D landscape</button><button id="landscape-reset" type="button">Reset view</button><button id="landscape-share" type="button">Copy place link</button></div>
    <p id="landscape-description" class="landscape-description">Cape Fear River and downtown Fayetteville. This inland river city is upstream of Wilmington.</p>
    <div class="coastal-globe-shell landscape-globe"><div id="coastal-globe" aria-label="3D terrain, aerial imagery, and available buildings at the selected location"><div class="globe-placeholder"><span>35.05° N<br/>78.88° W</span><small>FAYETTEVILLE / CAPE FEAR RIVER</small></div></div></div>
    <div class="landscape-footer"><p id="coast-globe-status" role="status">Open the landscape to load terrain and buildings.</p><p>Drag to orbit · Scroll to zoom · Ctrl + drag to tilt</p></div>
    <p class="fineprint">Building coverage and height detail vary by location. These 24 camera destinations provide geographic context. The ocean model covers three regions; the Tessera sample below covers the Cape Peninsula.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="coastal" class="coastal-section">
    <header class="section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">02 / TESSERA REPRESENTATIONS · CAPE PENINSULA · 2024</p><h2>The Cape Peninsula<br/><em>in 128 dimensions.</em></h2><div><p>Select a reference on the coast. Tessera’s full feature vector becomes a spatial query: which other locations resemble it in the satellite representation?</p><p>Use a second reference to inspect the difference. The colors show similarity or preference between references, with an explicit numerical scale.</p></div></header>
    <button type="button" id="landscape-tessera">View this sample in 3D</button>
    <div id="coastal-microscope" aria-live="polite"><p class="coastal-placeholder">The coastal representation loads when this section enters view.</p></div>

  </section>
  <section id="cinema" class="cinema-section"><header><p class="eyebrow">03 / CURRENTS IN MOTION</p><h2>A year of ocean flow.</h2><p>A text-free seasonal loop through NOAA’s monthly mean currents from satellite-tracked drifters. The drogue sat at 15 m. The colors show annual-mean speed; particles follow a visual integration through the climatology, not individual observed drifters. This is a long-run seasonal pattern, not today’s ocean.</p></header><div class="cinema-frame"><video id="ocean-film" controls preload="none" playsinline poster="/hidden-rivers/film-global-currents-poster.jpg" aria-label="Text-free seasonal global current visualization derived from NOAA drifter observations"><source src="/hidden-rivers/film-global-currents.mp4" type="video/mp4"/></video></div><div class="presentation-film-actions"><button type="button" data-present="film">Present clean film</button><a href="/hidden-rivers/film-global-currents.mp4" download>Download global film</a></div><div class="secondary-film"><p class="eyebrow">THE AGULHAS · HYCOM · 0 M</p><h3>One day along the current off southern Africa.</h3><p>A regional model view with the ocean surface kept level. Trails show horizontal motion only; particle count is not transport volume.</p><video controls preload="none" playsinline poster="/hidden-rivers/film-presentation-poster.jpg" aria-label="Text-free modeled surface flow in the Agulhas Current"><source src="/hidden-rivers/film-presentation.mp4" type="video/mp4"/></video><a href="/hidden-rivers/film-presentation.mp4" download>Download Agulhas film ↗</a></div></section>
  <section id="methods" class="methods-section"><header><p class="eyebrow">04 / METHODS & EVIDENCE</p><h2>Data and<br/><em>calculations.</em></h2></header><div class="method-intro"><p>The ocean model supplies eastward and northward velocity at geographic coordinates and depth. The atlas turns those fields into three distinct calculations. A path describes where a tracer travels; vorticity describes local rotation; finite-time stretching describes separation of nearby trajectories over a stated interval.</p><p>None is a direct observation of every parcel. The model grid, input cadence, missing values, and integration window constrain the result. Smaller integration steps improve the numerical calculation, not the resolving power of the source data.</p></div>
    <div class="method-ledger"><details open><summary><span>01</span> Geographic particle trajectories <span>+</span></summary><div class="method-body"><div set:html={math(String.raw`\frac{d\lambda}{dt}=\frac{u}{R\cos\phi},\qquad\frac{d\phi}{dt}=\frac{v}{R}`)}></div><p>Longitude λ and latitude φ are in radians; u and v are in metres per second. The renderer uses deterministic releases and a fixed physical sampling interval for trajectory history. The same model time therefore produces the same path geometry across devices. Masked cells and exits terminate trajectories.</p><p>Depth remains fixed at the selected model level. The oblique view uses the same horizontal and vertical scale, so deep layers sit close together. A 20 m rendering offset keeps paths legible against the surface. Particle number, glow, and line density aid visibility and do not represent transported volume or probability. NOAA relief and the model’s wet mask are independently gridded and can differ near slopes.</p></div></details>
    <details><summary><span>02</span> Rotation on a sphere <span>+</span></summary><div class="method-body"><div set:html={math(String.raw`\zeta=\frac{1}{R\cos\phi}\left[\frac{\partial v}{\partial\lambda}-\frac{\partial(u\cos\phi)}{\partial\phi}\right]`)}></div><p>Relative vorticity has units of inverse seconds. Positive values describe counterclockwise local rotation; negative values describe clockwise rotation. A diverging palette places zero at its center. This diagnostic is a finite difference of the model field; it does not by itself identify a persistent eddy.</p></div></details>
    <details><summary><span>03</span> Finite-time stretching <span>+</span></summary><div class="method-body"><div set:html={math(String.raw`\sigma_T=\frac{1}{2|T|}\log\lambda_{\max}\!\left[(D\Phi_T)^\mathsf{T}D\Phi_T\right]`)}></div><p>The flow map Φ moves nearby initial locations through 48 hours of velocity data. Its spatial derivative measures how those locations separate. The largest stretching direction gives the forward finite-time Lyapunov exponent, reported per day. Trajectories without a valid full integration window are masked.</p><p>High stretching can arise from shear as well as features associated with coherent transport. A bright ridge is not proof of a transport barrier. The published diagnostics include numerical sensitivity checks; they do not quantify all ocean-model uncertainty.</p></div></details>
    <details><summary><span>04</span> Similarity across all Tessera dimensions <span>+</span></summary><div class="method-body"><div set:html={math(String.raw`s(a,x)=\frac{e_a^\mathsf{T}e_x}{\|e_a\|\,\|e_x\|},\qquad\Delta s=s(a,x)-s(b,x)`)}></div><p>Each vector contains 128 learned features. The microscope compares these vectors by cosine similarity. Positive contrast means the location resembles reference A more than B in this representation. It does not certify habitat type, ecological health, water depth, or current velocity.</p><p>The annual coastal representation and ocean analysis have different dates and purposes. Their link is geographic. No predictive or causal relationship between embeddings and currents is claimed.</p></div></details></div>
    <div class="sensitivity-study"><div><p class="eyebrow">A MEASURED LIMIT / AGULHAS SURFACE</p><h3>The sampling interval changes the answer.</h3><p>For the same 48-hour calculation, switching from three-hour inputs to daily samples changes the stretching field much more than halving the integration step. Small numerical error does not establish physical accuracy.</p><p>Absolute FTLE differences are in day⁻¹. Each comparison uses seed/window pairs valid in both runs across four release dates. These are sensitivity checks, not observational error estimates.</p></div><div class="sensitivity-table"><table><caption>Agulhas surface · absolute change in 48-hour FTLE</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Changed setting</th><th scope="col">Median</th><th scope="col">95th percentile</th><th scope="col">Pairs</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Inputs: 3 hours → 24 hours</th><td>0.113692</td><td>0.427221</td><td>1,231</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">RK4 step: 900 s → 450 s</th><td>0.00000462</td><td>0.00005767</td><td>1,238</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Perturbation: ¼ → ⅛ cell</th><td>0.008695</td><td>0.056488</td><td>1,238</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The Depth view uses daily inputs for all five Agulhas layers. Surface-only transport and diagnostics retain the three-hour series. The six other velocity layers have daily inputs.</p></div></div>
    <div class="source-ledger"><div><h3>Inputs</h3><a href="https://www.hycom.org/dataserver/espc-d-v02/global-analysis">HYCOM / ESPC-D-V02 ↗</a><a href="https://www.aoml.noaa.gov/phod/gdp/mean_velocity.php">NOAA Global Drifter Program climatology ↗</a><a href="https://coastwatch.pfeg.noaa.gov/erddap/griddap/etopo180.html">NOAA ETOPO1 relief ↗</a><a href="https://geotessera.readthedocs.io/en/stable/">Tessera / GeoTessera ↗</a><a href="https://cesium.com/platform/cesium-ion/">Cesium ion terrain & buildings ↗</a></div><div><h3>Inspect the release</h3><a href="/hidden-rivers/data/manifest.json">Velocity metadata ↗</a><a href="/hidden-rivers/data/diagnostics.json">Diagnostic metadata ↗</a><a href="/hidden-rivers/data/source-requests.json">Daily source requests ↗</a><a href="/hidden-rivers/data/source-requests-3hour.json">Three-hour source requests ↗</a><a href="/hidden-rivers/data/source-requests-deep.json">500 m & 2,000 m source requests ↗</a><a href="/hidden-rivers/coastal/manifest.json">Tessera provenance & validation ↗</a><a href="https://github.com/ihelfrich/ihelfrich.github.io/tree/main/scripts/hidden-rivers">Analysis, tests & rendering code ↗</a></div><div><h3>Interpretation</h3><a href="https://os.copernicus.org/articles/21/401/2025/">FTLE uncertainty: Matuszak et al. ↗</a><a href="https://www.whoi.edu/ocean-learning-hub/ocean-topics/how-the-ocean-works/ocean-circulation/currents-gyres-eddies/">WHOI: circulation and eddies ↗</a><p>Research visualization. Physical validation against colocated observations remains outside this release.</p></div></div>
  </section>
